---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "clean simple eats protein powder",
      "clean simple eats protein",
      "cse protein powder",
      "clean simple eats review"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Best Tasting Picks",
    link: "/best-tasting-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Clean Simple Eats Protein Powder: Worth the Hype?</Fragment>
      A dessert-flavored, stevia-sweetened whey isolate with a loyal following. Here is what the label actually gives you, which tub to buy, and when a cheaper whey makes more sense.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is Actually in the Tub</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Lineup</a>
      <a href="#which-one">Which Product to Buy</a>
      <a href="#new-formula">The New Formula and Smaller Tubs</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Certifications</a>
      <a href="#who-skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#compare">How It Compares</a>
      <a href="#how-to-use">Getting the Most From It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Clean Simple Eats sells a good-tasting, naturally sweetened whey isolate, but it is a 20 g protein that is priced like a 25 g one. Buy it for the flavors and the short ingredient list, not for value, and check the serving count on the tub before you pay.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats is a Utah company that started as a meal-plan and recipe business and moved into supplements in 2018, according to a profile of co-founders Erika and JJ Peterson in Utah Business. Its protein powder is the product most people find it through, usually by way of a recipe or a social post showing a dessert-style flavor such as Chocolate Brownie Batter, Mint Chocolate Cookie or Cake Batter.
          </p>

          <p>
            The core product is simple to describe. Each scoop delivers about 20 g of protein from grass-fed whey protein isolate, in roughly 110 calories, sweetened with stevia rather than sucralose, with a small digestive enzyme blend added, per the label and retailer listings at the time of writing. That is a clean, light formula, and buyer feedback on taste is mostly positive, which is the reason the brand has the following it does.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is the dose and the price together. Twenty grams is on the low side for a whey isolate, where 25 g per scoop is common, and Clean Simple Eats charges a premium price for it. That pushes the cost per 25 g of protein well above mainstream wheys. You are paying for flavor, a stevia-only sweetener system and a brand you like, and that can be a perfectly good reason to buy, as long as you know it is the reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our short version: if you have tried artificially sweetened proteins and hated the aftertaste, or you want a light whey for baking and overnight oats, Clean Simple Eats is a reasonable pick. If you are trying to hit a high daily protein target on a budget, it is not the efficient choice, and our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best tasting protein powder</a> guide lists options that taste good and give you more protein per dollar.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A good-tasting light whey isolate, priced for the brand</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">About 20 g of grass-fed whey isolate in roughly 110 calories, stevia-sweetened, with a recent reformulation that some buyers dislike. Worth it for flavor-first buyers. Skip it if cost per gram of protein is what you care about.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Is Actually in the Tub
          </h2>

          <p>
            The protein source in the standard Clean Simple Eats powder is grass-fed whey protein isolate, which retailer listings describe as non-GMO and soy-free. Isolate is the more filtered form of whey, with less lactose and fat than concentrate, so it is a sensible base for a low-calorie shake. The Simply Vanilla label lists about 20 g of protein in a 33 g scoop, with 0 g of fat, about 9 g of total carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar and 0 g of added sugar, per the HEB and Walmart listings at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Calories land around 110 per scoop for Simply Vanilla and Chocolate Brownie Batter, and database listings put some flavors slightly higher, at about 120. Sodium varies more than you might expect: retailer labels show roughly 180 mg in Simply Vanilla and about 230 mg in Chocolate Brownie Batter. None of that is a problem in a normal diet, but it is worth knowing if you are tracking sodium closely.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweetener is stevia leaf extract, and some retailer summaries mention monk fruit in certain flavors, so read the panel on the flavor you buy. There is no sucralose or acesulfame potassium, which is the main draw for people who get a lingering aftertaste or stomach upset from artificial sweeteners. Several flavors also contain coconut, which the FDA treats as a tree nut for allergen labeling, so anyone with a coconut allergy should check before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            The added digestive enzyme blend is listed at about 100 mg per serving, made up of amylase, lipase, cellulase and protease, per the SuppCo label summary, with individual amounts not disclosed. That is a modest dose. It will not hurt, but it is not a reason to choose this powder over another isolate, and isolate is already low in lactose for most people. Treat the enzymes as a minor extra rather than a feature.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Label figure</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Simply Vanilla (per scoop)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it means</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower than the 25 g common in isolates</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Light; good for cutting and baking</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 g, 0 g added</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sweetness comes from stevia</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein source</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grass-fed whey isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low lactose, low fat</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Clean Simple Eats Lineup, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are the Clean Simple Eats protein products we could confirm on retailer listings at the time of writing, flagship first, followed by two alternatives from other brands for buyers who want more protein per scoop or a lower cost. Figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Clean Simple Eats Product to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most people the answer is the standard whey isolate in a tub, in whichever flavor you are most likely to finish. Simply Vanilla is the safest first buy because it works in shakes, oats, yogurt, pancakes and protein desserts, and it is the flavor that tends to show up on Amazon with the most buyer feedback. Chocolate Brownie Batter is the brand's other signature flavor and the better pick if you mostly drink your protein with milk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Single-serve stick packs, sold in 10-count boxes, carry the same 20 g of protein and are useful for travel, the gym bag or trying several flavors before you commit to a tub. They cost more per serving than the tub, so do not make them your everyday supply. The variety pack is the cheapest way to find out whether you like the dessert flavors, which matters because buyer opinion on individual flavors is more divided than on the brand as a whole.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats Clear Protein is the third option. It is also whey isolate at about 20 g per serving, but it mixes into a thin, juice-like drink in fruit flavors such as Blue Bahama and Pineapple Passion, sweetened with stevia extracts and colored with ingredients such as spirulina, per Target's ingredient listing. If you find milky shakes heavy after training or in hot weather, a clear whey is worth a look, and our <a href="/best-clear-protein-powder/">best clear protein powder</a> guide compares it with the wider category.
          </p>

          <p>
            We could not confirm a current plant-based or vegan Clean Simple Eats protein on the brand's main retail listings, so vegans should look elsewhere. Some third-party pages describe one, but the details conflict and we would not rely on them. If you need a dairy-free shake with a similar clean-label pitch, start with our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> list instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="new-formula" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The New Formula and the Smaller Tubs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats has reformulated its protein recently. Target listings now describe a new formula without xylitol or xanthan gum, and some flavors are explicitly labeled xylitol-free. On paper that is an improvement: xylitol is a sugar alcohol that causes bloating and loose stools in some people, and it is highly toxic to dogs, which matters if your scoop ever ends up on the kitchen floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buyer feedback on the change is mixed. Some Target reviewers say the new formula tastes and mixes differently, a few report a stronger aftertaste, and a number of long-time customers say they preferred the old version. Others are happy with it. Because taste was the brand's main selling point, this is worth weighing, and it is one more reason to buy stick packs or a variety pack before a full tub if you are new to the brand or returning after a while.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bigger practical change is the package size. Older tubs and the SuppCo listing show 30 servings, while current Target listings show tubs of roughly 17.6 to 21 oz with 20 servings, and several reviewers complain that the bag shrank without a matching price cut. Whether or not the price per serving moved for your retailer, always compare the serving count, not the sticker price, when you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this makes the product bad. It does mean that older reviews, including glowing ones from a few years ago, may describe a powder that is no longer on the shelf. Weight recent buyer reviews more heavily than older ones, and if a retailer is still selling old-formula stock, check the label and expiry date so you know which version you are getting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Price per tub tells you almost nothing about protein value. The useful number is what you pay for each 25 g of protein, which is a typical serving target. Because a Clean Simple Eats scoop holds about 20 g, you need about 1.25 scoops to reach 25 g, and that is where the brand's premium shows up.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the list price we saw on a 30-serving tub, roughly $60, a scoop costs about $2.00, which works out to roughly $2.50 per 25 g of protein. Amazon discounts have brought the same tub closer to $45 at times, or about $1.50 a scoop and roughly $1.90 per 25 g. Those figures are approximate, and prices move often, so recheck the current listing and the servings count before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            For comparison, large tubs of mainstream whey blends such as Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard often land at well under half that cost per 25 g at regular retail prices, and even premium isolates with 25 g or more per scoop frequently come in lower. Clean Simple Eats is not a budget protein in any sense, and buying stick packs pushes the cost per gram higher still.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the budget matters, there are two honest approaches. One is to use Clean Simple Eats as a flavor booster, mixing half a scoop with half a scoop of an unflavored whey. The other is to save it for recipes where its flavors do the most work and drink a cheaper whey day to day. Our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> guide covers the lower-cost end.
          </p>

