        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Testing and Certifications
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats says its products go through third-party testing, and retailer listings describe multi-stage testing of raw materials and finished product for pathogens, heavy metals and gluten. SuppCo notes ISO 17025-accredited testing behind the product but also says product-specific testing is not verified on its platform. We did not find published certificates of analysis for individual lots.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also could not confirm NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice certification for Clean Simple Eats protein at the time of writing, and we found no listing in either database. That matters only for a specific group: competitive athletes who are drug tested should use a product carrying one of those certifications, because they screen for banned substances lot by lot. For everyone else, it is a nice-to-have rather than a deal breaker.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the heavy-metal question, whey isolate is generally one of the lower-risk protein sources, because dairy proteins tend to test lower for lead and cadmium than plant proteins and cocoa-heavy blends. Chocolate flavors of any brand tend to carry more than vanilla because cacao naturally accumulates these metals from soil. If that is a concern, choose a vanilla or fruit flavor and ask the brand for recent test results.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our general rule, explained in more depth in <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a>, is that a brand claim of third-party testing is worth less than a named certification or a published lab report you can read. Clean Simple Eats sits in the middle: it makes the claim and describes its process, but you cannot independently check a certificate yet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Skip Clean Simple Eats
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are chasing muscle gain on a budget. A lifter aiming for 160 g of protein a day will notice the difference between 20 g scoops at a premium price and 25 g scoops at a mainstream price very quickly, and the extra money buys flavor rather than results. Muscle protein synthesis responds to the total protein and leucine you eat, not the brand on the tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you dislike stevia. Stevia has its own aftertaste, which some people describe as bitter or licorice-like, and since it is the main sweetener here, a stevia-averse buyer will not be won over by a different flavor. If you are unsure, buy a stick pack first, or pick a sucralose-sweetened option from our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> ranking.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are vegan, have a milk allergy or need a coconut-free product, because the standard line is dairy-based and several flavors contain coconut. Skip it too if you compete in a drug-tested sport, since we could not confirm an NSF or Informed Choice certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, if you have kidney disease, are pregnant, take medication or have a significant food allergy, speak to your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding any protein powder. Nothing on this page is medical advice, and a protein shake is a convenience food, not a treatment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How It Compares With Other Flavor-First Proteins
          </h2>

          <p>
            The closest rival in spirit is Ghost Whey, which also sells on flavor, through licensed dessert and cereal collaborations. Ghost gives you more protein per scoop, about 25 g per its label, but uses non-nutritive sweeteners that include sucralose in many flavors. If you are fine with sucralose, Ghost usually wins on protein per dollar. If you want stevia only, Clean Simple Eats has the cleaner sweetener story. Our sibling <a href="/ghost-protein-powder-review/">Ghost protein powder review</a> goes into that range in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Transparent Labs Grass-Fed Whey Isolate is the better comparison for stevia fans who want a higher dose. It also uses stevia, carries more protein per scoop, about 28 g per its label depending on flavor, and the brand publishes lab results. It costs more per tub, but often less per 25 g of protein than Clean Simple Eats, which makes it the upgrade pick if you like the clean-label idea but want efficiency.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against plant-based flavor favorites such as Orgain, Clean Simple Eats has the edge on protein quality and calories, because whey isolate delivers a complete amino acid profile with plenty of leucine in a small number of calories. Against cheap mainstream wheys, it loses on cost and wins on sweetener choice. Where it sits for you depends on which of those trade-offs you care about most.
          </p>

