        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Getting the Most From Clean Simple Eats
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because the brand grew out of recipes, its powders are designed to be cooked and blended, and that is where they earn their price. Stir a scoop into Greek yogurt, overnight oats or a mug cake, and you add about 20 g of protein without much extra fat or sugar. Whey isolate can turn rubbery if baked at high heat for long, so mix it into batters with some moisture and pull baked goods a little early.
          </p>

          <p>
            For shakes, the flavors taste richer with milk than with water, but milk adds calories and protein of its own. Eight ounces of skim milk adds roughly 8 g of protein, which takes a single scoop to about 28 g. That is a simple way to hit a 25 g serving without paying for a second scoop, and it improves the texture too.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are using protein powder to help with appetite during a cut, the light calorie count is a real advantage. A scoop with water, ice and a little cinnamon is a low-calorie snack that keeps you fuller than most sweets. Pair it with whole-food protein through the day rather than leaning on shakes for everything; our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide shows how to set a daily target.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, rotate flavors with care. The dessert flavors are fun, but buyers who drink protein every day sometimes tire of sweet profiles. Keeping a tub of Simply Vanilla as the daily driver and a second flavor for treats is the setup most likely to keep you from abandoning a half-full tub.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing tubs by sticker price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clean Simple Eats tubs have moved between 30 and 20 servings depending on the version and retailer. A cheaper-looking tub can cost more per serving. Divide the price by servings, then adjust for the 20 g dose, before deciding whether a deal is actually a deal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating one scoop as a full serving</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                One scoop gives about 20 g of protein, not the 25 g many people aim for after training. If you want 25 g, use about 1.25 scoops or mix it with milk. Otherwise you are quietly under-dosing every shake while paying a premium for it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting old reviews of the old formula</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The brand reformulated to remove xylitol and xanthan gum, and buyers report changes in taste and texture. Reviews from a few years ago may describe a different powder. Read recent reviews first, and try stick packs before committing to a full tub.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the coconut allergen</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Several Clean Simple Eats flavors, including the clear line, contain coconut, which US labeling treats as a tree nut. People with a coconut allergy should read every flavor's allergen statement, because it varies across the range and between the tub and stick formats.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it for drug-tested sport</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                We could not confirm NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice certification. A brand testing claim is not the same as lot-by-lot banned-substance screening. Competitive athletes who are tested should choose a certified product and check the certifier database for the exact flavor.
              </p>
            </div>
