        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Clean Simple Eats protein powder good?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is a good-tasting, light whey isolate. Each scoop gives about 20 g of grass-fed whey protein in roughly 110 calories, sweetened with stevia, per the label at the time of writing. Buyer feedback on flavor is mostly positive. Its weak point is value: the 20 g dose and premium price make it expensive per 25 g of protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Clean Simple Eats protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  About 20 g per scoop in the standard whey isolate, per the label at the time of writing, with the clear protein also listed at about 20 g. Figures vary slightly by flavor. To reach a 25 g serving, use about 1.25 scoops or mix one scoop with milk, which adds roughly 8 g per cup of skim milk.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What sweetener does Clean Simple Eats use?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The protein is sweetened with stevia leaf extract, and some retailer listings mention monk fruit in certain flavors. It contains no sucralose or acesulfame potassium. The newer formula also drops xylitol, a sugar alcohol that bothers some stomachs. Check the label on your flavor, because ingredients can differ across the range.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Clean Simple Eats protein whey isolate or concentrate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The standard powder and the clear line are both listed as grass-fed whey protein isolate, the more filtered form of whey. Isolate is lower in lactose and fat than concentrate, which suits light shakes and many people with mild lactose sensitivity. People with a true milk allergy should avoid it entirely.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Clean Simple Eats protein third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The brand says its products are third-party tested for pathogens, heavy metals and gluten. We could not confirm NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice certification at the time of writing, and we did not find published lot certificates. Tested athletes should pick a certified product. Most other buyers can treat this as a minor gap.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Clean Simple Eats good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can help as part of a calorie deficit, because a scoop delivers about 20 g of protein in roughly 110 calories, and protein helps with fullness. No powder causes weight loss on its own. Use it to replace a higher-calorie snack, and talk to a doctor or dietitian if you have a medical condition or take medication.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Clean Simple Eats protein sold on Amazon?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Clean Simple Eats whey isolate tubs, stick packs and variety packs are listed on Amazon, and the brand also sells through Target, Walmart, HEB and its own site. Compare the serving count across listings, because package sizes have changed, and recheck the flavor's label on the listing before you order.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

